🧪 Hypothesis Testing (Quick Context)

Hypothesis testing helps determine whether an observed result is **statistically significant** or occurred **by chance**.

Key components:

* Null Hypothesis (H₀)
* Alternative Hypothesis (H₁)
* Significance level (α)
* Test statistic
* P-value / Critical value

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Z-Test

What is a Z-Test?

A **Z-test** is used to test hypotheses about a population mean or proportion **when population variance is known** and the sample size is large.

---

When to Use Z-Test?

* Population standard deviation (σ) is known
* Sample size **n ≥ 30**
* Data is approximately normally distributed

---

Types of Z-Tests

* One-sample Z-test (mean)
* Two-sample Z-test (means)
* Z-test for proportions

---

Z-Test Statistic

```
Z = (x̄ − μ) / (σ / √n)
```

---

Key Points

* Based on standard normal distribution
* Uses Z-table
* Rare in real-world problems (σ usually unknown)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 T-Test

What is a T-Test?

A **T-test** compares means when the **population variance is unknown** and the sample size is small.

---

When to Use T-Test?

* Population standard deviation unknown
* Sample size **n < 30**
* Data approximately normally distributed

---

Types of T-Tests

1️⃣ One-Sample T-Test

* Compares sample mean with population mean

2️⃣ Independent (Two-Sample) T-Test

* Compares means of two independent groups

3️⃣ Paired T-Test

* Compares means of the same group before & after treatment

---

T-Test Statistic

```
t = (x̄ − μ) / (s / √n)
```

Where:

* s = sample standard deviation

---

Key Points

* Uses **t-distribution**
* Degrees of freedom (df) matter
* Very common in real-world analysis

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Chi-Square Test

What is a Chi-Square Test?

A **Chi-Square (χ²) test** is used to test relationships between **categorical variables**.

---

Types of Chi-Square Tests

1️⃣ Chi-Square Test of Independence

* Checks if two categorical variables are related

2️⃣ Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit Test

* Checks if observed data matches expected distribution

---

When to Use Chi-Square Test?

* Data is categorical
* Observed vs expected frequencies
* Large sample size preferred

---

Chi-Square Statistic

```
χ² = Σ (Observed − Expected)² / Expected
```

---

Key Points

* Uses χ² distribution
* Cannot be used for continuous data
* Sensitive to sample size

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 ANOVA (Analysis of Variance)

What is ANOVA?

**ANOVA** tests whether there is a **significant difference between the means of three or more groups**.

---

Why Not Multiple T-Tests?

* Increases Type-I error
* ANOVA controls error rate efficiently

---

Types of ANOVA

1️⃣ One-Way ANOVA

* One independent variable
* Compares multiple group means

2️⃣ Two-Way ANOVA

* Two independent variables
* Examines interaction effects

---

ANOVA Hypotheses

* **H₀**: All group means are equal
* **H₁**: At least one mean is different

---

ANOVA Statistic (F-Test)

```
F = Variance between groups / Variance within groups
```

---

Key Assumptions

* Normal distribution
* Homogeneity of variance
* Independent samples

---

Important Note

ANOVA tells **if a difference exists**, not **where**.
(Post-hoc tests are needed for that.)

---

📏 Confidence Intervals

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What is a Confidence Interval?

A **confidence interval (CI)** provides a **range of values** within which a population parameter is likely to lie.

---

Common Confidence Levels

* 90%
* 95% (most common)
* 99%

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Interpretation (Very Important)

> A 95% confidence interval means that **95% of such intervals** will contain the true population parameter.

⚠️ It does NOT mean there is a 95% probability the parameter lies in one specific interval.

---

Confidence Interval for Mean (σ known)

```
CI = x̄ ± Z(σ / √n)
```

---

Confidence Interval for Mean (σ unknown)

```
CI = x̄ ± t(s / √n)
```

---

Factors Affecting CI Width

* Sample size (↑ n → narrower CI)
* Confidence level (↑ confidence → wider CI)
* Variability in data

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Comparison Summary

| Test       | Data Type   | Sample Size     | Purpose           |
| ---------- | ----------- | --------------- | ----------------- |
| Z-Test     | Numerical   | Large (σ known) | Mean / proportion |
| T-Test     | Numerical   | Small           | Compare means     |
| Chi-Square | Categorical | Large           | Association       |
| ANOVA      | Numerical   | Any             | ≥3 group means    |
| CI         | Numerical   | Any             | Estimate range    |

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚠️ Common Mistakes

* Using Z-test when σ is unknown
* Using T-test for more than two groups
* Confusing correlation with causation
* Misinterpreting confidence intervals

